<header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">HUMAN GOLD RUSH LESSON</p><h1>The Tiger’s Eye Covenant</h1><p class="intro">A touchstone held in the hand can create a pause long enough to choose how we meet another person.</p><blockquote>What changes when we carry our values in the palm of our hand?</blockquote><div class="actions"><a href="human-gold-rush.html#welcome">Take the Covenant, Receive Your Stone<span>→</span></a></div><aside class="notice"><strong>A modern-day pet rock</strong><p>The Tiger’s Eye touchstone borrows the pet-rock premise on purpose — a plain object made meaningful by a story and a ritual, not by its shape.</p></aside></header><section class="content-grid"><article><span>01</span><div><h2>Practice, not perfection</h2><p>FAITH means Fair, Accepting, Insightful, Transparent, and Humble. The covenant asks for practice rather than agreement, conformity, or perfection.</p></div></article><article><span>02</span><div><h2>A physical reminder</h2><p>The Tiger’s Eye touchstone is not money, a ticket, or proof of virtue. It is a personal prompt to bring the covenant into everyday relationships.</p></div></article><article><span>03</span><div><h2>Gather</h2><p>Picking up your touchstone is simply a fulfillment center activity — no public pledge, no demonstration, no audience. Take the covenant privately, in your own time, and carry it home.</p></div></article><article><span>04</span><div><h2>Notice</h2><p>Notice whether a physical reminder changes the pause between feeling and action. Never grade another person’s sincerity.</p></div></article><article><span>05</span><div><h2>Twelve engraved touchstones</h2><p>The CEI pilot stocks twelve wholesaler-purchased tumbled palm stones, each engraved with a single word: Courage (Tiger’s Eye), Kindness (Obsidian), Wisdom (Howlite), Lucky (Unakite), Belief (Crazy Agate), Gratitude (Red Jasper), Health (Aventurine), Happiness (White Crystal), Dream (Yellow Jade), Believe (Sodalite), Wealth (India Agate), and Healing (Opal). Join the Rush lets you choose the word that speaks to you. Postcards, not ads, direct people to RSVP and pick one up at a local LAUGH fulfillment center — you are scheduled for pickup, not received by accident.</p></div></article><article><span>06</span><div><h2>A thirteenth stone, cut locally</h2><p>One touchstone doesn’t come from that wholesale order. The MEGAvoter’s Tiger’s Eye Worry-Prayer stone is lapidary-cut at CEI and engraved FAITH — a distinct piece from the wholesale Courage (Tiger’s Eye) stone any YAM’er can already choose. Same material, different stone, different word, different source.</p></div></article><article><span>07</span><div><h2>Two touchstones, two paths</h2><p>A YAM’er who takes the covenant to practice FAITH RSVPs free and is simply notified when and where to pick up a Practice FAITH touchstone — any of the twelve wholesale words, Courage (Tiger’s Eye) included — at a local LAUGH event, triggered by selecting a Peace Pentagon branch to support. A MEGAvoter’s $12/year pledge carries the locally cut Tiger’s Eye Worry-Prayer stone engraved FAITH, alongside a Peace Pentagon role and Organized Krill kit responsibilities. In practice, most members carry exactly two touchstones for the whole of their involvement — the one Practice FAITH word chosen as a YAM’er, and the FAITH-engraved Tiger’s Eye if they later become a MEGAvoter. This isn’t a set of thirteen to collect; there’s no expectation of returning for the other words.</p></div></article><article><span>08</span><div><h2>Repetition as its own practice</h2><p>For some, reserving and returning for a touchstone, again and again, may echo the rhythm of a rosary — repeated, physical, focused attention. Here that attention isn’t directed at a figure to petition, but at your own presence on earth: a reminder that showing up, again and again, is the whole practice. This is one possible personal lens, offered alongside — not in place of — however each person already prays.</p></div></article></section><section class="media-section"><div class="section-heading"><p>MEDIA FOR THIS LESSON</p><h2>Watch, listen, and discuss</h2></div><div class="media-grid"><article class="media-card video"><div class="media-frame"><span>▶</span><small>VIDEO 1</small></div><h3>The Tiger’s Eye Covenant: orientation</h3><p>Reserved for the featured story or introductory video.</p><a href="#">Transcript coming soon</a></article><article class="media-card video"><div class="media-frame"><span>▶</span><small>VIDEO 2</small></div><h3>Community conversation</h3><p>Reserved for a demonstration, conversation, or local example.</p><a href="#">Discussion guide coming soon</a></article><article class="media-card podcast"><div class="media-frame"><span>◉</span><small>PODCAST</small></div><h3>Listen and reflect</h3><p>Reserved for the facilitator or community podcast episode.</p><a href="#">Transcript coming soon</a></article></div></section>
